WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.630
 Delicious Revolution is brought to you by us, by me and Chelsea, specifically by our consulting business.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:11.600
 We provide participatory research, communications, and creative work to organizations that are building food movements.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.370
 What's your food movement? Let us know and get in touch.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.750
 DeliciousRevolutionShow.com

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:33.010
 Much better your dining experience would be if your server or your chef could build their craft over 15 or 20 years or a lifetime as opposed to moving from restaurant to restaurant to restaurant,

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.910
 continuously seeking better wages and working conditions for their families.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:44.510
 So, I think that's why right now we're seeing the biggest rift I've ever seen in the history of this industry,

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.710
 where all of these restaurants are moving towards us and towards the high road,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:55.010
 because they're recognizing what we're recognizing that, ultimately, if we want a better industry,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:01.210
 we need to treat these people as professionals, we'll have a better dining experience, we'll have better profitability as employers.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.410
 Everybody will succeed if we treat these people as the professionals that they are.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:14.940
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.020
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:17.630
 And I'm Devin Sampson.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.760
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of food,

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:28.180
 including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists, bringing you in-depth conversations with some amazing people.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.240
 On this third season of Delicious Revolution,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:35.020
 we're bringing you stories and perspectives from the unseen places in food systems,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.480
 going behind kitchen doors, into underground nests of native bees, under the waves,

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.360
 and to the far away origins of flavors we love, just to name a few.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:50.470
 I'm speaking with people who work with food in places we normally cannot see, or don't notice.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.080
 It's a season of unseen stories of food.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.220
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.420
 Just search for Delicious Revolution wherever you get your podcasts, and you'll find us.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:08.920
 Our website is deliciousrevolutionshow.com, where we have pictures, links, and more information about all the people on our show.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:20.530
 Saru Jayaraman is the co-founder and co-director of the Restaurant Opportunity Centers United, or ROC United,

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:25.350
 and she's the director of the Food and Labor Research Center at the University of California, Berkeley.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.450
 After 9-11, together with Displaced World Trade Center workers, she co-founded ROC in New York City,

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:35.590
 organizing restaurant workers to win workplace justice campaigns, conduct research and prefer policy work,

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:39.550
 partner with responsible restaurants, and launch cooperatively owned restaurants.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:43.670
 ROC now has 10,000 members in 19 cities worldwide.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:51.590
 Saru is the author of Behind the Kitchen Door, and more recently, Fort, a new standard for American dining.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.450
 Here's Chelsea's interview with Saru Jayaraman.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.440
 Well, let's start with, how did you start doing this work with restaurant workers?

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.930
 Well, it was really about 9-11.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:10.210
 I was an attorney and an organizer and actually had worked with some restaurant workers on labor issues,

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:14.610
 but on 9-11, there was a restaurant at the top of the World Trade Center, Tower One.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.590
 On that morning, 73 workers died in the restaurant, and about 250 workers lost their jobs.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.590
 And I was asked to start a little relief center in the aftermath of the tragedy,

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.450
 together with workers from Windows on the World, the restaurant at the top of the World Trade Center, survivors.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:33.570
 And so myself and Fekak Mamdou, a surviving waiter from the restaurant,

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.150
 started ROC as a little relief center, the Restaurant Opportunity Center.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.510
 And what started as a little relief center for restaurant workers post-9-11

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.810
 has grown 15 years later into a national organization of about 18,000 workers,

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:52.010
 about 200 restaurant owners, and several thousand consumer members in about 15 states across the country.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.400
 That's a lot to happen that fast.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.180
 Yeah, it was really because of the demand in this industry.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.740
 It's the second largest and absolute fastest growing sector of the U.S. economy.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.180
 It's over 11 million workers, but it's also the absolute lowest paying and the least unionized.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:14.740
 And so naturally, with such a large industry, the lowest wages in the country and nowhere to turn,

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:21.510
 the demand for our work just really drove the growth of the organization quite rapidly.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:28.610
 Yeah, I was really interested in the labor politics of whether you're a union or whether you're not a union

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:35.170
 and what, really what, you guys call it ROC, that's the, okay, Restaurant Opportunity Center.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.970
 What are all the things you do and what are the kind of workers that you represent?

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:41.900
 So no, we're not a union.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.540
 And part of the reason we're not a union is that we're actually not just a worker organization.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:50.460
 We're workers, employers, and consumers in this industry fighting for better wages and working

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.020
 conditions for restaurant workers that we see actually has positive impacts on employers and

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.500
 on consumers as well, which is why employers and consumers have joined forces with us to change

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.120
 the industry.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.400
 One of the things we do, we've done everything from campaigns to move restaurants that are

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:12.320
 breaking the law and taking what we call the low road to profitability, to move them to the high

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.480
 road, to pay back workers' wages and tips that have been stolen.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.860
 So we've recuperated like $10 million in stolen tips and wages for thousands of workers.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.100
 At the same time, we've promoted what we call the high road to profitability, and that has

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:29.020
 included creating a national association called RAISE of restaurant owners that are doing the

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.440
 right thing, providing good wages and good working conditions.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:36.060
 It's also included opening our own restaurants called Colors, one in New York, one in Detroit.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.020
 We're opening one here in Oakland pretty soon.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:42.600
 And in these restaurants, training thousands of low-wage workers to move up the ladder into

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:48.040
 livable wage, mostly fine dining server and bartending positions, and through that process,

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:50.780
 actually engaging them in the organizing and policy work.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.460
 And that's the third piece of what we've done over the last many years.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:54.940
 So much research.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.280
 You know, I teach at UC Berkeley.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.800
 We've worked with about 100 academics around the country.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.960
 We've published over 30 reports and three books on the industry.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.760
 That's been the basis for local, state, and federal policy work.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.220
 We've raised the minimum wage in a number of states.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.240
 We've won paid sick days for workers in a number of states.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.960
 And as you know, our current issue that we've been working on for the last several years is

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.360
 to eliminate the lower wage for tipped workers in this country, still stuck at $2.13 an hour.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:24.190
 Wow.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.220
 That's a lot that's happened in 15 years.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.660
 And it's because there's so many people involved in this industry, right?

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:30.920
 Yes.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.180
 So how many people are involved in this industry?

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.300
 So it's 11 million people in the restaurant industry.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.140
 One in 12 Americans currently works in the industry.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.460
 One in two of us has worked in this industry at some point in our lifetime.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:49.460
 For people who care about food, you know, the food system as a whole is one in six American workers.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.600
 The restaurant industry is more than half of that entire food system.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.060
 So 20 million workers in the entire food system, 11 million of which are in the restaurant industry.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:05.840
 The restaurant industry is by far the largest segment of the food system and the second largest industry in the whole economy overall.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.320
 And it just continues to grow.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:09.800
 I'm sure you heard the data.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:16.340
 Last year, we made world history becoming the first nation on Earth in which we now spend more money on eating out than we do on food eaten inside the home.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:24.090
 The amount with which we eat out, the amount of money we spend on eating out increases every year in astronomical numbers.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.750
 The industry is quite healthy and doing quite well.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:33.690
 And yet, despite all of that size and growth, it is the absolute lowest paying employer in the United States.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.830
 So every year, the U.S. Department of Labor puts out a list of the 10 lowest paying jobs.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:42.370
 Every year, seven of the 10 lowest paying jobs in America are restaurant jobs.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.890
 The two absolute lowest paying jobs in America are restaurant jobs.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.790
 The eighth lowest paying job is a farm worker position.

00:07:49.030 --> 00:08:00.430
 So I like to say if eight of the 10 lowest paying jobs in America are food jobs, seven of which are restaurant jobs, it's important for people who care about food to understand that the food system is not a bad employer.

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:13.330
 They are the absolute worst employer in the United States, and the restaurant industry in particular in the food system in general drags down the entire economy for all workers because we really set the floor.

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:30.920
 The reason we set the floor is that our industry is notorious among all low-wage workers because restaurants are represented in Congress and in every state legislature by a trade lobby called the National Restaurant Association.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.740
 We call it the other NRA.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:35.700
 They've been named the 10th most powerful lobbying group in Congress.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.900
 They're by far the most powerful employer lobby in the United States.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:57.400
 And they have lobbied vitriolically and spent millions of dollars to keep the minimum wage as low as, I say, inhumanely possible, not just for the restaurant industry because that's who they primarily care about, but it ends up being as low as possible for all workers.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.500
 And so that's how we set the floor.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.300
 The chains that sort of set the standards in our industry don't set the standards for our industry.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.840
 They set the standards for our country and our economy.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:17.120
 And so in this way, food, you know, it doesn't just impact our nation all the ways I think your listeners may think about from the fact that we all eat and nutrition and health.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:27.990
 It also is the biggest, frankly, floor, the biggest weighing down anchor on our economy as the lowest paying employer in the United States.

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:32.310
 And it's also the lowest common denominator in other ways, too, right?

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.850
 Restaurants are famous for hiring lots of undocumented immigrants.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.890
 And so there's this kind of invisible economy going on.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:42.850
 And can you talk about how those things all intersect within the work that you do?

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:51.190
 Yeah, I mean, I think the restaurant industry is the largest employer of everybody, not just immigrants, but also women and people of color and formerly incarcerated folks.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:54.450
 I mean, this is the industry that everybody works in.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.290
 As I said, one in two of us have worked in this industry in our lifetime.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:07.820
 So everybody has the potential, but there's incredibly severe racial segregation in our industry such that I said it's the lowest paying employer.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.200
 We estimate that about 20% of the jobs actually are livable wage jobs.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.780
 They're fine dining server and bartending positions that are growing.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:24.470
 You know, part of what's happening that I think your show is a part of is an explosion in foodie culture and the food interest in food.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:28.570
 And that has exploded fine dining as a segment of our industry.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:35.010
 And yet those fine dining jobs are held almost exclusively by white men, people of color, women of color.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:41.890
 Those folks with records, immigrants are not able to get up to those wonderful fine dining, livable wage jobs.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:48.810
 They're segregated into lower level segments like fast food and casual restaurants, and they're segregated into lower level positions.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:55.370
 So even if you find them in fine dining, you'll find them in the back of the house or in busser and runner positions.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:11:05.690
 You know, very much a color line that gets literally darker the further back into the restaurant you go.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:13.540
 You know, all of this has origins in our very racialized slave history in this country.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:19.640
 You may know that even the tip minimum wage of $2.13 an hour is a legacy of slavery.

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:22.910
 You know, because tipping didn't originate in the United States.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.410
 It originated in feudal Europe.

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:30.510
 And when it came to the States, it was rich Americans traveling to Europe in the 1850s and 1860s and coming back,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.750
 trying to show off that they knew the rules of Europe.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:39.670
 And when they did, there was a massive anti-tipping movement here in the States saying, you know, this is undemocratic.

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:40.550
 It's un-American.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:42.530
 Employers should pay their workers, not customers.

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:47.820
 And that movement spread to Europe and succeeded in Europe, which said, this is a vestige of the feudal system.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:48.780
 We reject it.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.530
 And which is why you see very little tipping in Europe today.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:12:01.290
 And here in the States, we actually mutated what happened in feudal Europe and made it much worse by saying, really, by the restaurant industry,

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.150
 arguing that they should have the right to hire newly freed slaves.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:08.850
 This is right after emancipation and not pay them anything and let them live on customer tips.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:14.900
 I mean, these were, after all, black, valueless human beings who hadn't been paid for centuries.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:22.920
 And so the idea of not paying them anything at all and letting them live on customer tips was a natural extension of where they had their value in society.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.300
 And that idea was codified into the very first minimum wage law that passed in the United States,

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.920
 which said you have the right to the minimum wage either through wages or through tips,

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:38.640
 making the restaurant industry the only industry now on earth that gets away with saying we shouldn't have to pay anything at all to our workers.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.660
 You, the customer, should pay our workers' wages for us.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:50.860
 So we went from a $0 minimum wage in 1938 to a whopping $2.13 an hour in 2016, a $2 increase over a 100-year period.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:57.540
 And the industry continues to argue that it should have to pay its own workers' wages, the industry would collapse.

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:02.940
 It disproportionately affects women of color who are the tipped workers working at the IHOPs and the Applebee's,

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.500
 the majority of where tipped workers work.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:14.090
 And all of this, from the very notion of a back of house and front of house to the idea that somebody could be paid $2 an hour,

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:18.690
 is all a legacy of a very racialized slave history in this country.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:27.800
 While I was reading your book and eating out, I was like, wow, this is, I mean, these are these things I've seen my entire life.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:32.110
 And, you know, I like to, like, fancy myself, like, fairly aware.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:35.510
 And I have, you know, done anti-racist organizing and all these things.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:40.120
 And it was this totally invisible thing that I ran into here.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:41.740
 We were traveling internationally.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:45.860
 I ran into other places and it just, I mean, it continues to blow my mind.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.860
 So, like, as a diner, what do I do?

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:50.100
 How do I respond to this?

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:51.620
 What conversation do I have?

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:55.820
 Because now, I mean, I've, while reading this book, you talk about having that conversation.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:58.940
 And I still feel, like, unsure of how to approach that.

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:06.240
 Well, you know, it's funny because after I wrote the first book, I went around the country and diners said, well, what can we do?

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:07.260
 Where should we eat?

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:08.200
 How can we support?

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:18.700
 And that is actually the pathway that led to this new book because we created a guide and an app that tells diners how restaurants fare on issues of wages, benefits, and promotions.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:29.780
 And our 200 restaurants around the country, from Danny Meyer and Tom Colicchio to Alice Waters, who are doing it right, get awards in our guide and our app and are profiled in the book.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:36.330
 There are just not enough of them that are doing it differently yet for you to only eat at those restaurants.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:43.490
 So it cannot just be you as a diner only eating at the restaurants that get the high road seal of approval.

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:46.950
 You would not eat out very much or you'd keep eating in the same places.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:58.830
 And so instead, we need you as diners to approach your favorite restaurants, wherever they are, whoever they are, the owners and the managers, not the workers, and say, I love the food.

00:14:58.930 --> 00:14:59.870
 I love coming here.

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:00.890
 I love the service.

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:06.050
 I want you to know as a regular customer, it's very important to me that you take the high road to profitability.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:13.850
 You may be good on local and organic, but I need you to also be sustainable in other ways to the people in this restaurant.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:20.530
 And so I encourage you to join the restaurant association that's taking the high road called Ray's.

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:29.630
 And actually, over the last year, this is how Alice Waters actually joined Ray's, was because a diner said you really need to consider joining this high road association.

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:33.870
 Blue Bottle Coffee joined over the last year because of consumers.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:39.930
 So consumers have tremendous power to tell companies, we think you should follow a different path.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:42.710
 And it's been working over the last many years.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:48.640
 You know, we worked very closely with Danny Meyer as he moved to eliminate tipping in his restaurants.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:16:00.140
 And that process was in part driven by aggregating a lot of consumer support for him making a pretty dramatic move.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:13.000
 So I think consumers can play a huge role in speaking to your favorite restaurant owners and managers, letting them know that they should, you know, join the high road employer association, Ray's, be in touch with us.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:25.020
 We also encourage diners to just read the book because it's important to get educated on these issues and share that information with others and ultimately to join us in fighting for policy change.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:31.280
 So we have a website called onefairwage.com, which is all about getting rid of this lower wage for tipped workers.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:37.740
 We're asking people to go to that site and input their information and it'll help you send a letter to your legislator saying,

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:40.660
 we need to abolish this legacy of slavery as a consumer.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:45.040
 I don't want to have to subsidize these multimillion dollar corporations with my tips.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:53.480
 One of the things that I also thought about while reading your book is like working in rural Mexico, a big conversation there is about immigration, right?

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:58.840
 And so people really talking to us all the time about like, should I go, right?

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:06.010
 And then people who have come back who talk about the jobs they had and where we worked, it was, there were a lot of cooks, right?

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:18.950
 So we worked in Southern Mexico, which, you know, and they're mostly men who immigrated, who had never cooked once when they lived in Mexico and then came here and were professional cooks.

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:29.880
 And, and some of them moved back and some cook and some don't cook, but, but it was like this, such an interesting thing to me about, you know, these are very dear people to me.

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:37.070
 And there's such dignity in this work and I feel, and I'm not alone in this because they're, everybody eats out all the time.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.430
 So taken care of by these people all the time.

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:55.030
 And yet I don't, because of this front of the house, back of the house thing, there's not the visibility and there's not that relationship in the same way that I feel like has really exploded with farm workers and, you know, even meat packing plants or these other things.

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.770
 They're kind of much less glamorous than cooking food.

00:17:57.990 --> 00:17:59.310
 So that felt really interesting.

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:08.430
 Well, I, I, it's interesting because, um, I think there's a disconnect, not just with the kitchen and the back of the house, but with all, all of these workers are completely invisible.

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:10.070
 In fact, they're trained to be invisible.

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:17.910
 The whole idea is you're the invisible hands that clear the table and anticipate the customer's needs before they even know that they need them.

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:34.170
 Uh, so it's not, but I, I think what's important is the more we recognize these individuals as skilled professionals and we pay them and treat them as the skilled professionals that they are, whether you're a server or a cook or a bartender, you are a skilled professional.

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:41.630
 And in other countries, you know, people go to school for several years to be hospitality professionals, and that's how we should view these workers.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:42.790
 They are professionals.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:48.230
 The more that we recognize people as professionals, the better dining experience we'll actually have.

00:18:48.370 --> 00:19:03.050
 Because if you could allow somebody to build their profession, staying in one place, a place that provides livable wages, rather than experiencing the kind of turnover that our industry experiences, which is the highest turnover, has the highest turnover rate of almost any industry in the United States.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:18.360
 Think how much better your dining experience would be if your server or your chef could build their craft over 15 or 20 years or a lifetime, as opposed to moving from restaurant to restaurant to restaurant, continuously seeking better wages and working conditions for their family.

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:46.040
 So I think that's why right now we're seeing the biggest rift I've ever seen in the history of this industry, where all of these restaurants are moving towards us and towards the high road, because they're recognizing what we're recognizing, that ultimately, if we want a better industry, we need to treat these people as professionals, we'll have a better dining experience, we'll have better profitability as employers, everybody will succeed if we treat these people as the professionals that they are.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:55.390
 I'm just thinking about how funny it would be if you treated the people in your family, like as disposable as, you know, whoever cooks and cleans and cares for you at home.

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:59.690
 It's so funny because every restaurant owner likes to say, oh, it's fine, we're a family.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:16.750
 And we always say, gosh, it's such a dysfunctional family, because, you know, what kind of a family pays people $2 and exploits them to the point that they can't survive, can't take care of themselves, experience severe mental and health issues as a result of that.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:35.780
 Well, and then talking to Emma Rosenbush from Cala, she was talking about this practice of hiring formerly incarcerated people, and then they're just not being the resources to support them being hospitality professionals, right?

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:37.620
 So maybe you can talk about that a little bit.

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:38.780
 What are those resources?

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:40.880
 What do you guys offer for employers?

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:43.280
 Because everybody needs help in it, right?

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:44.180
 Everybody needs help, yeah.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:21:01.260
 Well, on the training side, we have a pretty comprehensive training program that helps workers advance, as I said, from coming out of prison or fast food or being a busser or dishwasher up a ladder into fine dining service and bartending.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:10.380
 So we provide introduction to customer service, advanced fine dining service, all to help people move up a ladder into these livable wage jobs.

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:26.500
 And for employers, we also provide training and technical assistance about how they could actually improve their wage structures, how they could desegregate their restaurants so that they have more people of color in the front, how they could provide childcare and all these other things.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.780
 So we have a training and technical assistance program.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:36.800
 And most importantly, a peer community of, like I said, 200 fabulous restaurant owners that are able to help one another learn.

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:43.480
 So your favorite restaurant owner that, you know, is just following the old standard, we don't think it's malicious, you know.

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:45.720
 We think it's that they don't know a different way.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.420
 And joining a peer community like this helps put them on a road.

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:50.800
 And that's the key point.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:53.100
 The high road, as we call it, is a road.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:54.140
 It's not a destination.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:55.240
 It's a pathway.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:58.300
 It's something that we're continuously improving upon.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:21:59.240
 Nobody's perfect.

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:12.660
 So I think it's all about helping people start down the path of paying people better, treating them better, desegregating, considering all the human factors that could lead to better professionalization of this industry.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:17.780
 And as long as they're willing to continuously do better by their workers, we are with them.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:31.780
 That seems like such a challenge to the labor movement as well, and one that I think people who have worked in kind of old school labor politics, it's so adversarial all the time.

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:36.160
 But I think it's very alienating for both sides at a certain point.

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:39.100
 So how did that decision come about?

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:46.120
 Like how, you know, that you don't just, you know, you have to come together, right, rather than just fight the whole time.

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:55.930
 Yeah, well, for us, it was necessary because for so long in our industry, the notion had been that it's impossible.

00:22:55.930 --> 00:23:00.730
 It's impossible to run a restaurant and pay people well and treat people well.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:16.430
 And I mean, there's been this such mythology put out by the most powerful restaurant associate, powerful employer association in America, mythology that there's only one way to run a restaurant and that we have the smallest profit margins of any industry in the United States.

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:18.730
 And thus, there's no way to pay people better.

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:22.110
 And so we knew that there was no way to succeed.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:34.040
 Given the power of this industry, there was no way to succeed except to divide the opposition, except to actually lift up the restaurant owners who are doing it right.

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:43.880
 And we realized it couldn't just be about having a one-time relationship with these employers where, you know, we worked with them once and they came with us and they said, no, I can do it.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:58.040
 And then, you know, it had to be about building a relationship with them over years and years and years and helping them move even as employers, pushing them to do even better than they were already doing and pushing ourselves to do better as well.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:01.230
 And so, you know, we've opened our own restaurants.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:02.130
 We're not perfect.

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:07.690
 And so we've learned through our own experience that it's hard and we have sympathy.

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:12.250
 And so we just found to win on these issues.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:21.050
 There was no other way except to build real, long-lasting, positive relationships with a group of employers who could set a different path.

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:27.490
 And I would say over the last year that those 10 years of work of building that up finally paid off.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:45.880
 And we saw over this last year between Danny Meyer and Tom Colicchio and the book coming out, just as I said, the first time in 15 years where suddenly this high road path is visible, viable, real, very high profile, even trendy.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:52.580
 It had always been a dream for us to make livable wages and working conditions as trendy, as sustainable and local.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:56.360
 And I think it's actually becoming that for the first time ever.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.530
 That's really exciting.

00:24:58.710 --> 00:24:58.950
 Yeah.

00:24:59.070 --> 00:24:59.810
 Good job.

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:00.950
 Congratulations.

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:02.350
 Keep up the good work.

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:12.630
 Something I was thinking about when you were talking about that, though, is that the only uniform thing I can think about restaurants is that they serve food to people.

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:15.470
 I mean, I was a restaurant worker.

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:16.590
 I probably will be.

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:27.470
 Again, it's just likely when you, you know, restaurant workers are an amazing group of people because they're also musicians and artists and all the rest of these people who make your life rich in so many ways.

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:32.660
 But, you know, there's nothing standard about a restaurant, right?

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:34.100
 They're all run different ways.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:41.010
 And so it seems like relationships would be the only way that that would be possible because it's also not transparent.

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:41.670
 No.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:46.640
 You know, like one IHOP is not the same as another IHOP, weirdly enough.

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:47.440
 Right?

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:47.900
 Yeah.

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:53.680
 So one of the things I really loved in both of your books is all the case studies, right?

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:57.040
 I was like, why is she using case study after a case study?

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:59.320
 Because it's so anecdotal in this certain way.

00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:06.430
 But maybe you could, it really proved this point of that it's like, it's not standard, right?

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:09.530
 The things that people struggle with in different places.

00:26:09.530 --> 00:26:17.500
 Of course, there's, there are these set of things, wages, sick days, safety conditions, hours, those kinds of things.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:30.200
 But there's also like, and safety, like, I think in terms of personal safety, you know, especially like in terms of the dynamics, whatever the family dynamics of the restaurant are.

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:39.720
 But can you talk through maybe a few of these high road and low road cases and especially this Darden thing that you guys are working on right now?

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:47.240
 Yeah, so in the book, I profile, let's take, every chapter is a different segment of the industry.

00:26:47.240 --> 00:27:05.920
 So it's true, restaurants are incredibly diverse, but we're so fortunate in that I have found a feeling of unity and camaraderie among food service workers in all segments and all different kinds of restaurants that I don't necessarily see in other industries.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:15.560
 There's something, I don't know, similar to going through the military or, you know, that people feel this kinship with other people in this industry.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:21.710
 Because there's something about hospitality and food service that does bring people together.

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:23.030
 It's a kind of personality.

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:27.480
 But anyway, every chapter of the book is a different segment.

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:41.540
 And so, for example, we have a chapter on casual restaurants and we profile Vimla's Curry Bossom Cafe, which is a restaurant in North Carolina that's gotten a lot of attention for being locally sourced and organic, really delicious Indian food.

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:45.560
 But I think few people knew she also treats her workers really well.

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:57.520
 In a state that allows tipped workers to be paid $2.13 an hour, she's paying $10 and $11 starting wage, even for workers like bartenders who could be paid as little as $2.13 an hour.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:03.840
 So, she is already taking the high road, but willing to do even better.

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:06.680
 I mean, we've worked with her on a number of issues in her own restaurant.

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:14.290
 And we compared Vimla's as a casual restaurant, very family-oriented restaurant.

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:16.430
 Her entire family works in that restaurant.

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:25.140
 To another restaurant that's all about family, which is the Olive Garden, which puts itself, I mean, its motto is we're all family here, you know.

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:28.360
 And Vimla really does embody family, you know.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:30.340
 It's her entire family working in the restaurant.

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:32.600
 Her workers are treated like family.

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:36.240
 And when I say treated like family, they're paid anywhere between $10 and $20 an hour.

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:41.820
 She has workers who make more than she does, which is important to recognize.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:46.600
 She provides child care reimbursements for workers, which is unheard of in our industry.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:50.400
 And just across town in North Carolina, we visited the Olive Garden.

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:52.920
 The worker was making $2.13 an hour.

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:55.040
 The motto is we're all family here.

00:28:55.120 --> 00:28:58.040
 And this is the company that doesn't just pay $2.13.

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:05.960
 They are lobbying vitriolically as the world's largest employer of tipped workers to keep the wage at $2.13 an hour.

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:17.900
 And they are the case study for how this issue of the tipped minimum wage is the source and the reason that this industry has the highest rate of sexual harassment of any industry in the United States.

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:25.790
 So in the book, I describe a worker at the Olive Garden who worked there for many years, was a single mother.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:35.950
 There was a maitre d' in the restaurant who was notorious for touching, grabbing, groping, harassing all the female servers.

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:38.470
 Anybody who complained was let go.

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:47.670
 Even though the company has a quote-unquote zero tolerance policy for sexual harassment, what it really means is a zero tolerance policy for complaining about sexual harassment.

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:50.010
 That's what zero tolerance really means at the Olive Garden.

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:53.220
 So it's just the epitome of the opposite.

00:29:53.220 --> 00:30:04.970
 And again, both people are using the word family, but you have a dysfunctional family and then something that I think really embodies family in the sense of the most extensive family possible.

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:11.950
 Not even just your own nuclear family, but the community as family, your consumers as family, the workers as family.

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:17.310
 I mean, it's just a totally different concept and the contrast is quite striking.

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:28.600
 I mean, it's really amazing because I, well, I did catering for a long time, which I think is like the bad cousin of restaurant work or something.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:33.200
 It's like if you can't work in a restaurant, you work as a caterer.

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:36.460
 And of course, all those things are really true there too.

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:48.150
 But there's so many, I don't know, I'm getting a little tongue-tied thinking about all these things in conjunction with each other.

00:30:48.570 --> 00:30:54.200
 There's so many places to start in terms of like what struggle looks like there.

00:30:54.420 --> 00:31:00.540
 And it's so, we all know these examples of these great restaurants that do treat their workers, right?

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:07.790
 Like they're not, they're not all Chez Panisse and they're not all Tom Colicchio, even though like that's so great that they're part of it.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:10.890
 So maybe you could, I mean, Vimla's is one of those.

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:12.190
 Yes, who are the others?

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:19.450
 You know, I think of lots of like breakfast places and these places that are just part of your town.

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:20.490
 Maybe your taqueria.

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:28.260
 I mean, I don't know, like, but where a lot of things have necessitated another labor model, right?

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:32.000
 So maybe one of the ones I really like in your book is these restaurants.

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:34.430
 What was, the one in LA.

00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:35.310
 What's her name?

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:35.990
 Oh, Deeps.

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:36.650
 Yeah, Deeps.

00:31:36.850 --> 00:31:37.650
 Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:38.230
 Yeah, yeah.

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:40.390
 Good girl, I met.

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:41.640
 That's right.

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.940
 So it's a Vietnamese food restaurant, right?

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:49.330
 And it like comes out of, she just loved to cook, that it sounds like, right?

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:57.610
 And then she kept trying to figure out how to make her world as a labor organizer and a cook come together over time.

00:31:57.610 --> 00:31:58.810
 Is that what happened?

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:00.070
 Yes, I think that's true.

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:01.930
 She wasn't a labor organizer, but she was an organizer.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:10.810
 And she definitely, yeah, found her path opening a restaurant and, you know, I think has really tried to bring her social justice values into the restaurant.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:13.040
 But I don't even think it takes that.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:21.560
 I mean, we've got mom and pop restaurants around the country who just come in saying, this whole system doesn't make sense to us.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:24.080
 You know, in no other industry are you allowed to do this.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:27.660
 So we're just going to treat people better because it's the right thing to do.

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:37.880
 So I profile a company called Moo Cluck Moo in Michigan, which has been compared a lot to McDonald's because it is quick service.

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:43.320
 It is burgers and fries and chicken and yet he pays $15 an hour.

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:44.740
 He's got a tiny little store.

00:32:44.740 --> 00:32:49.580
 He does not see making burgers or making chicken as unskilled work.

00:32:49.660 --> 00:32:52.440
 He sees it as very skilled artisan craft work.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:54.740
 And he's growing.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:56.380
 He's opening new stores.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:57.840
 He's doing really well.

00:32:58.580 --> 00:32:59.560
 Everybody's investing.

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:01.400
 And he's paying people really well.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:17.560
 He started as a little mom and pop restaurant or the Florida Avenue Grill in Washington, D.C., which, you know, very the epitome of the greasy spoon diner, literally, you know, with all the pictures on the walls of the people who have visited the restaurant.

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:27.300
 I mean, it's just the epitome of a diner that you expect to pay people very, very little and yet totally committed to taking the high road and doing the right thing.

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:37.980
 Or Zingerman's in Michigan, you know, just known for really good food, you know, internationally known as a great deli paying workers, what they call a thrivable wage.

00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:39.300
 Don't even believe in a livable wage.

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.220
 They want to pay people a thrivable wage.

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:52.170
 Or even here in Oakland, Adrian is the owner of Kingston 11, a Jamaican restaurant that is great food but also just committed to treating people better.

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:57.050
 So there are so many examples of small mom and pop restaurants.

00:33:57.050 --> 00:34:12.830
 You're right, it's not the Danny Myers and the Tom Colicchio's but all of these folks collectively prove it doesn't matter what segment of the industry you're in, what kind of restaurant you operate, what the food is or where you come from, you can follow the high road and we can help you get there.

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:37.480
 Well, because that's what I thought when I was reading that like, oh, it can only be the Tom Colicchio's and it can only be these, you know, sort of high end, whatever, sort of sustainably and fashion would have trendily committed to whatever kind of the thing of the moment is until you started breaking that down and looking at what those components that went into that.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:47.450
 So I guess another thing that came up was, you're talking about these individual businesses, what would it look like for Darden to change the direction that they face?

00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:51.290
 Would it be like a huge shift in things or would it be a few degrees?

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:53.390
 And how does that happen on different scales?

00:34:53.890 --> 00:35:22.940
 Yeah, I mean, ultimately, it has to be about policy change, you know, it's it can't be about individual restaurants or even huge companies like Darden, just changing their practices in their one company, it has to be about policy change, it has to be about policy change for so many reasons, you know, our high road restaurants talk about, it needs to be a level playing field, you know, for for they can't be sticking their necks out doing the right thing, and everybody else gets to pay $2.13 an hour.

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:32.420
 It has to be policy change, because we don't actually just need high road companies, we need high road cities, states, we need a high road nation, we need a high road nation.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:47.280
 And so we're starting to see with the fight for 15 and all these incredible successes around the country, whole cities and states moving to $15 moving to provide paid sick days moving to even, you know, require health insurance.

00:35:47.480 --> 00:36:01.980
 I think this is the direction we need to go, not just for the sake of the workers, but even for the employers who are doing it right, and for all employers, because I can sympathize, it would be hard to stick your neck out and pay a lot more than everybody else.

00:36:02.140 --> 00:36:12.220
 But if everybody has to do it, it helps you in so many ways, A, you're not alone, but B, all these workers across the entire economy, everybody's wages are going up.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:14.980
 And what does that mean, more consumption dollars for you?

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:29.300
 Because everybody has to remember that low wage workers, minimum wage workers spend way more of a percentage of their income on consumption, on eating out, than people at the upper end of the scale.

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:44.320
 And so raising wages at the bottom is one of the best ways to support this industry because, and we've seen it, you know, the cities and states with higher minimum wages have actually faster job growth rates in this industry.

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:57.060
 The seven states that have completely eliminated the lower wage for tipped workers and require the restaurant industry to pay the same wage to everybody, tipped and non-tipped, have, there's California, Oregon, Washington, Nevada, Minnesota, Montana, and Alaska.

00:36:57.580 --> 00:37:06.940
 These seven states have higher restaurant sales per capita, higher job growth in the industry, higher job growth among servers and tipped workers, even higher rates of tipping.

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:10.900
 We tip better on the West Coast than the 43 states with lower wages for tipped workers.

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:26.370
 So if you listen to the Restaurant Association, you'd think anybody who pays these workers at all a livable wage would have no industry, would have no jobs, jobs would be lost, everybody would be replaced by robots, or there'd be no restaurants at all, and nobody would tip.

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:31.550
 All of those things, the opposite has been proven to be true in the seven states.

00:37:31.550 --> 00:37:39.990
 And it's just so obvious that it's because when workers, you know, get paid better, they provide better service, they can spend more.

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:41.310
 It just works.

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:47.870
 The Fight for 15 has been so inspiring to see unfold.

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:54.940
 And last year I participated in this thing with SEIU with the adjunct, you know, adjunct professors and the Fight for 15.

00:37:55.140 --> 00:38:04.950
 That was like such a conceptually interesting thing to be a part of, of thinking about what tenuous labor looks like across these fields.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:09.490
 And that feels really interesting about the work that you're doing at ROC too.

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:19.000
 Maybe let's talk about some ways people can get involved with all of those things and what that might mean for their local communities to kind of wrap it up.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:24.990
 And also, like, if they're interested in your new book is Forked, it just came out a couple of months ago.

00:38:25.230 --> 00:38:26.810
 It's got a lot of good examples.

00:38:27.490 --> 00:38:29.770
 And then we can leave it there because I know you've got to run.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:31.520
 Definitely.

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:36.470
 So if you're a worker, employer, or consumer, you can get involved.

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:42.230
 If you're a worker, just go to rockunited.org, check out where our local offices are, or sign on as an online member.

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:47.610
 If you're an employer, please consider joining RAISE, our High Road Employer Association.

00:38:47.610 --> 00:38:50.430
 You can find it on our website, rockunited.org.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:55.330
 And if you're a consumer, you can become a member of our consumer association, Diners United.

00:38:56.110 --> 00:39:00.050
 You can go to my book website, forkedthebook.com.

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:05.630
 You can find an app for consumers that tells you how restaurants are faring on these issues.

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:09.490
 You can go from there to join Diners United, the Consumer Association.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:15.290
 And as a member of the Consumer Association, you can be a different kind of diner.

00:39:15.290 --> 00:39:20.170
 You can talk to your employer, to your favorite restaurant owner about joining RAISE.

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:29.760
 You can actually download and print out little cards that are on that website that you can leave with your tips to let workers know that there's a place

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.260
 they can go for help.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:34.420
 It drives them to the onefairwage.com website.

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:39.120
 And most importantly, as a consumer, you're not just a consumer, you're a citizen.

00:39:39.400 --> 00:39:47.100
 And so you should go to the onefairwage.com website, put in your zip code, and send a letter to your representative saying,

00:39:48.100 --> 00:40:03.640
 "I believe that this industry should pay one fair wage like every other industry, and I don't want as a consumer to have to subsidize this industry by paying its workers' wages in my tips and by paying for its workers' survival to the tune of $16.5 billion annually in taxpayer-funded public assistance, whether that's food stamps or Medicaid, for workers in this industry who just can't survive, even though they work full-time."

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:17.140
 So we are doubly subsidizing this industry as consumers and we have a real interest in making this industry pay for its own workers.

00:40:17.140 --> 00:40:18.140
 Perfect.

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:19.140
 Thanks so much.

